## Artefact 1: EU AI Act (2024)

### Summary  
The EU Ai Act (2024) was and still is recognised as the first comprehensive legal framework designed to regulate the use of AI across the EU. It categories AI systems into 4 risk levels, those being unacceptable, high, limited and minimal. It also places strict requirements on high risk systems used in areas such as healthcare, policing and employment. The act also bans the use of harmful AI such as social scoring and manipulative biometric surveillance while also requiring transparency for systems such as chatbots and deepfakes. It also mandates things like human oversight, data quality standards and accountability mechanisms for organisations deploying AI. The act was released in 2024 and has become a global standard and reference point.

### Reflection  
I chose this as an artefact as it directly links to the units focus on governance, accountability and ethical responsibility in ICT practice. Furthermore, the act helped me understand how ethical principles such as transparency, fairness and harm prevention are translated into real policies. It also helped highlight for me the importance of anticipating risks before deploying AI systems, especially in high risk environments. This artefact is meaningful because it demonstrates how governments attempt to balance innovation with societal protection. It also encouraged me to reflect on how similar regulatory approaches may shape the future of ICT work in Australia.


